  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{({x}^{2}-1)^{2}-1}{{x}^{2}}=$",
  "worked_solution": "If we plug in $x=0$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so  we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{({x}^{2}-1)^{2}-1}{{x}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[({x}^{2}-1)^{2}-1]}^{\u2032}}{{[{x}^{2}]}^{\u2032}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{4x({x}^{2}-1)}{2x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{4{x}^{3}-4x}{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{({x}^{2}-1)^{2}-1}{{x}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{4{x}^{3}-4x}{2x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{[4{x}^{3}-4x]^{\u2032}}{[2x]^{\u2032}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{12{x}^{2}-4}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{12(0)^{2}-4}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4}{2} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
